<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

A row tells you what is true now. An enterprise also has to answer *what happened, who did it, and when*: for audit, for debugging (lesson 14's step 4), and as the input every later layer consumes. Projections (16), compliance (18) and process triggers (21) all read **the event log**, not the tables.

`ash_events` records every action on an audited resource as an event row, in the same transaction as the write. In `ash_enterprise` you do not add it per resource: **batteries are inherited.** `AshEnterprise.Platform.Resource` adds `AshEvents.Events` pointing at the one central log, `AshEnterprise.Audit.EventLog`, unless a resource opts out with `audit?: false`, a local, greppable decision.

## The tutorial domain and the Refund

Create these two files.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial.ex
defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial do
  use Ash.Domain, otp_app: :ash_enterprise

  resources do
    resource AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund do
      define :propose_refund, action: :propose, args: [:ticket_ref, :amount]
      define :approve_refund, action: :approve
      define :reject_refund, action: :reject
    end
  end
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial/refund.ex
defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund do
  use AshEnterprise.Platform.Resource,
    domain: AshEnterprise.Tutorial,
    ownership: :organization_owned,
    lifecycle?: false

  postgres do
    table "tutorial_refunds"
    repo AshEnterprise.Repo
  end

  attributes do
    attribute :ticket_ref, :string, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
    attribute :amount, :decimal, allow_nil?: false, public?: true, constraints: [greater_than: 0]
    attribute :reason, :string, public?: true
    attribute :status, :atom,
      constraints: [one_of: [:proposed, :approved, :rejected]],
      default: :proposed, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    create :propose do
      accept [:ticket_ref, :amount, :reason]
    end

    update :approve do
      validate attribute_equals(:status, :proposed)
      change set_attribute(:status, :approved)
    end

    update :reject do
      validate attribute_equals(:status, :proposed)
      change set_attribute(:status, :rejected)
    end
  end
end
```

Three steps, and the order matters. First create the files above, then add `AshEnterprise.Tutorial` to `config :ash_enterprise, ash_domains: [...]` in `config/config.exs` — the codegen task discovers domains through that list. Then, in a normal terminal (not the attached IEx; these are mix tasks and boot their own node), generate the migration and apply it:

```bash
devenv shell -- mix ash.codegen --dev
devenv shell -- mix ash.migrate
```

`--dev` names the migration for you, which is what you want while learning — codegen generates, migrate applies; the overview says when to name one for real. Finally **restart the app node** (Ctrl-C twice, then the overview's start command) and reconnect Livebook's runtime, re-running the actor/tenant cell below once you are reattached. Application env is read at boot, so no amount of `recompile()` will register a domain that was added after the node started. Notice there is no `uuid_primary_key`, no `organization_id`, no timestamps and no `events do` block. Ask your agent (lesson 14) where they came from:

```elixir
AshAgentTools.describe_resource(AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund)
|> get_in([:attributes])
|> Enum.map(& &1.name)
```

## Write, then read the log

Set up an actor and tenant from the seeded data:

```elixir
require Ash.Query
actor = Ash.read_first!(AshEnterprise.Accounts.User, authorize?: false)
tenant = actor.organization_id
opts = [actor: actor, tenant: tenant]
```

Propose a refund of `25.00` for ticket `"T-1001"`, then approve it.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4"></i>Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  refund = AshEnterprise.Tutorial.propose_refund!("T-1001", Decimal.new("25.00"), opts)
  refund = AshEnterprise.Tutorial.approve_refund!(refund, opts)
  ```

  If this is forbidden, that is lesson 14: `AshAgentTools.can(AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund, :propose, actor)` and `explain_forbidden/2` tell you which inherited policy said no.

  </div>
</details>

Now read the events for that record. The event log is a resource, so you query it with Ash:

```elixir
AshEnterprise.Audit.EventLog
|> Ash.Query.filter(record_id == ^refund.id)
|> Ash.Query.sort(occurred_at: :asc)
|> Ash.read!(actor: actor, tenant: tenant)
|> Enum.map(&{&1.action, &1.action_type, &1.data})
```

Two events: `:propose` with the accepted inputs, `:approve` with the change. Each also carries the actor, the tenant and `metadata["correlation_id"]`, which ties together all the events one request produced.

## The rules of the log

* **Only actions write events.** That is why lesson 11 insisted actions are the only door. An `Ecto` update around the action leaves no event, and every later layer will be wrong without knowing it.
* **The log is append-only and tamper-evident.** `ash_enterprise` hash-chains it per tenant (ADR 0020), riding on the advisory lock `ash_events` already takes.
* **Replay re-runs actions.** Changes still execute during replay. So nothing non-deterministic belongs in a `change`: no HTTP, no `DateTime.utc_now/0` you did not capture as input, and never a model call (you will see this as a law in lesson 22). Put remote calls in generic actions (lesson 13) whose *results* are recorded as inputs.

## Exercise: name the event

Events are as meaningful as action names. Add `update :request_info` that accepts `:reason` and keeps status `:proposed`. Call it, then confirm the log says `request_info`, not `update`.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4"></i>Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  update :request_info do
    accept [:reason]
    validate attribute_equals(:status, :proposed)
  end
  ```

  Add `define :request_refund_info, action: :request_info` to the domain. A generic `:update` with a status field would record *that something changed*; a named action records *what the business did*. Projections and process triggers key on these names.

  </div>
</details>

When you are finished with the whole tutorial, the overview's **When you're done** recipe rolls back this lesson's migration and reverts its config line and snapshots.

